      <div class="prose prose-kasra max-w-none lg:max-w-[46rem]">
        <h2 id="why-links">Why links stop mattering the moment a model answers</h2>
        <p><strong>Backlinks score 85 for SEO and 10 for AI citations because PageRank is a Google calculation, not a language-model calculation.</strong> Google crawls the link graph, scores every page by who links to it, and uses that score as one of its strongest ranking inputs. That has not changed, whatever the last conference talk told you.</p>
        <p>An AI assistant answering a question does something different. It rewrites the question into a handful of searches, retrieves candidate passages, and then chooses which ones to quote based on how well each passage answers the question. The link score of the page that passage came from is not part of that choice.</p>
        <p>I have watched this play out on client sites. A page with a Domain Rating in the seventies and 400 referring domains lost the citation to a two-year-old blog post with 12 referring domains, because the smaller site stated the answer in one paragraph and the larger site spread it across a 3,000-word guide with no direct sentence to lift.</p>
        <p><strong>What replaces PageRank for AI is the context around the link.</strong> A model reads the sentence your link or mention sits in and learns what you are from it. A link from a Domain Rating 90 site in a footer teaches it nothing. A mention on a small blog that says who you are, where you are and what you are known for teaches it exactly what to cite you for. That is why link context scores 70 for AI while PageRank scores 10, and why the same outreach campaign can move Google and do nothing for AI, or the reverse.</p>
        <p>Understanding how each assistant retrieves is the starting point, and the differences between them are covered in the guide to <a href="/seo/ai-and-seo/ai-search-engines/">how AI search engines rank answers</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="why-context">Why context takes over</h2>
        <p><strong>Context scores 85 for AI citations because a model cites passages, and a passage has to carry its own meaning to be citable.</strong> When you read a guide, you carry the earlier sections with you. A model retrieving a chunk of your page does not. It gets the chunk, judged on its own.</p>
        <p>That is why "as mentioned above" and "this approach" are citation killers. The model cannot resolve what "this" is, so the passage fails the relevance check and a competitor's clumsier but complete paragraph wins.</p>
        <p>Context has three parts, and the chart scores each of them as a separate factor because you can fix them separately:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The passage is self-contained.</strong> Entity named, claim stated, reason attached, inside one block.</li>
          <li><strong>The claim is quotable.</strong> A short factual sentence a model can lift without editing.</li>
          <li><strong>The entity is consistent.</strong> The same facts about you appear in the same words across your site and across the web, so the model can attribute the claim with confidence.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Notice what is missing from that list: the entity. An assistant runs a search, pulls chunks that match the question and synthesises an answer from them. Most of the time it is not checking who you are. That is why entity co-occurrence and entity consistency, which are core SEO work at 70 and 65, sit at 40 and 45 for AI. They shape which chunks come back for questions about you or your category, but the passage does the heavy lifting.</p>
        <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-[14px] border border-tint-border bg-tint p-5">
          <p class="font-display text-[0.65rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-dim">A semantic triple, highlighted</p>
          <p class="example-text mt-2 text-lg font-semibold leading-relaxed text-ink"><span class="hl-sub">Kasra Dash</span> <span class="hl-pred">recovers</span> <span class="hl-obj">websites from Google algorithm updates</span>.</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-1 text-xs font-semibold">
            <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted"><span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-sm bg-[#b7a3ff]"></span>Subject: the entity</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted"><span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-sm bg-[#5eead4]"></span>Predicate: what it does</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted"><span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-sm bg-[#fbbf24]"></span>Object: what it does it to</span>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">One entity, one verb, one object, in one sentence. That is the unit a model lifts and attributes. Every example in the factor breakdown below is built the same way.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Google scores the first two at 35 and 40 and entity consistency at 65. It benefits from them, particularly for featured snippets and AI Overviews, but it will rank a page without them if the links and the topical cluster are strong. If you want the Google-side version of this, the guide to <a href="/seo/ai-and-seo/ai-overviews-optimisation/">optimising for AI Overviews</a> covers how Google chooses what it lifts.</p>

        <h2 id="factor-by-factor">Every factor, and why it scores the way it does</h2>
        <p>Each entry below is the reasoning behind the two numbers on the chart. Each one carries a worked example with the part that matters highlighted. Read the ones with the biggest gap first. Those are where your existing SEO effort is either wasted for AI or where you are doing nothing at all.</p>
        {sorted.map((f) => (
          <div id={f.id} class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-[20px] border border-tint-border bg-tint p-6 scroll-mt-28">
