        <h2 id="strategy">What to change if you want both</h2>
        <p><strong>The same page can rank in Google and get cited by AI, because the two systems mostly disagree on weight rather than on direction.</strong> Nothing on the AI side of the chart hurts your Google rankings. The work is additive.</p>
        <p>Here is the order I run it in on client sites:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Keep the link programme running.</strong> It still decides Google, and Google still sends most of the traffic. Do not trade it for AI work.</li>
          <li><strong>Rewrite the sections that matter as self-contained answers.</strong> Start with the pages that already rank. Each H2 gets a bolded direct answer in its first two sentences, with the entity named.</li>
          <li><strong>Fix your entity facts everywhere they appear.</strong> Same name, same location, same titles, same dates on your site, your author pages, LinkedIn, Crunchbase and any profile a model might read. This is mostly for Google, which ranks you against its Knowledge Graph entry for you. Put the facts in visible copy, not just schema, because the schema does not get read.</li>
          <li><strong>Engineer co-occurrence on purpose.</strong> Decide the three or four entities you want to be associated with, then make sure every bio, guest appearance, roundup entry and interview puts your name in the same sentence as them. This is core SEO work that defines the entity for Google, and it shapes which chunks the assistants retrieve for questions about your category.</li>
          <li><strong>Change what you ask for in link outreach.</strong> Stop chasing the strongest domain and start chasing the right sentence. A mention that describes who you are and what you do, on a relevant page, is worth more to a model than a bare link from a stronger site.</li>
          <li><strong>Get into the roundups.</strong> Search your category with "best" in front of it in ChatGPT and Perplexity, note which lists it cites, and get onto them. This is the fastest lever for category-level recommendations I have found.</li>
          <li><strong>Put dates on everything and keep them honest.</strong> A visible last-updated date, backed by real changes, moves the freshness score for the retrieval-based assistants.</li>
          <li><strong>Check who you are blocking.</strong> Open robots.txt and confirm GPTBot, PerplexityBot, ClaudeBot and Google-Extended are not disallowed unless you have decided that on purpose.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Google's own documentation on <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features" rel="noopener" target="_blank">AI features and your website</a> confirms there is no separate optimisation for AI Overviews beyond standard indexing and helpful content, which is exactly why the Google-side AI system sits closest to classic SEO on the chart.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">What I see go wrong</h2>
        <p><strong>Treating AI visibility as a content-volume problem.</strong> Sites publish 200 new pages to "cover the topic for AI" and none of them get cited, because every page is written in the same dependent, page-level style as before. Ten rewritten sections beat 200 new pages.</p>
        <p><strong>Chasing a single score.</strong> The 0 to 100 numbers on this page are weights, not targets. A page does not need 95 on context; it needs the passage that answers the question to stand on its own.</p>
        <p><strong>Building schema for AI.</strong> Teams spend weeks on JSON-LD entity graphs because a conference talk said models read them. In testing they do not. Schema is worth doing for Google's rich results, but if the facts are not in the visible copy, the model never sees them.</p>
        <p><strong>Ignoring the retrieval gate.</strong> Teams spend a quarter on entity work while robots.txt has been blocking GPTBot since 2023. Check the gate before touching anything else.</p>
        <p><strong>Assuming Google AI Overviews behave like ChatGPT.</strong> They do not. AI Overviews draw from the Google index and lean on the same signals as the classic results, so a page that ranks has a head start. ChatGPT and Perplexity retrieve from elsewhere and reward the AI-side factors more heavily. The guide to <a href="/seo/ai-and-seo/ai-quality-rater-guidelines/">how Google's raters judge AI-era content</a> shows how far Google's own quality standard has moved towards first-hand experience, which is the one factor both systems agree on.</p>
        <p><strong>Letting the numbers on your own site disagree.</strong> A founding year on the about page that differs from the one on LinkedIn is enough for a model to hedge. Consistency is cheap and most sites still have not done it.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions About SEO Ranking Factors vs AI Citation Factors</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
